WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.156 --> 00:00:01.290
This is the Steve Young

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.030
you most likely remember.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.370
He was a defining athlete
of his generation,

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.760
building a career out
of defying expectations.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.770
From being eighth on the BYU depth chart,

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.020
to a record USFL contract,

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.600
to sitting behind the great
Joe Montana for years,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.660
Young ultimately became a Hall
of Fame, Super Bowl winning

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.920
quarterback.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.290
Known for his pinpoint passing accuracy,

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.390
and a penchant for dynamic runs.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.370
Trouble, he's gonna be
sacked, no, gets away!

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.998
He runs, gets away again!

00:00:28.998 --> 00:00:33.219
To the 35, to the 20, to the 15, the ten.

00:00:33.219 --> 00:00:35.386
He dives, touchdown 49ers.

00:00:36.253 --> 00:00:38.280
I played 18 seasons.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.660
There's a few that have played more.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.420
Tom Brady's now 23, but I got as many

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.250
as maybe anyone who ever played.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.530
And I still did have half of my life,

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.880
that I wanted to be productive.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.710
And what could you do with
another half of a life?

00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:53.910
Turns out, a lot.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.140
Steve Young didn't just make

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.840
a name and a fortune from his NFL days.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.840
Armed with a law degree earned
while he was still a player

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:01.890
and a front row seat

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.930
to the tech revolution in Silicon Valley,

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.390
he's quietly built in an
investment firm that's done

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.890
more than $50 billion in deals
over the past two decades.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:14.520
I mean, look, my previous
life, it was amazing.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.200
You know, throwing Jerry Rice a touch down

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.900
in front of 80,000
people and winning games.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.350
Like there's nothing like it.

00:01:21.766 --> 00:01:25.560
It's not 80,000 people watching
it, but it feels familiar.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.250
And I think that's why there
is magic in private equity.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.890
This is the Steve Young many haven't seen:

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.750
the player turned investor.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.100
I recently got to spend a few days

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.900
trying to keep up with Young,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.840
from his offices in Silicon
Valley to a Scottsdale golf club

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.150
and lots of places in between.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.840
What started as a straightforward
story about an athlete's

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.868
successful transition to business,

00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:49.677
turned out to be something much more.

00:01:49.677 --> 00:01:51.810
The tale of a relentless guy

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.240
intent on finally marrying his
playing and investing careers

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.360
with an eye toward radically changing

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.903
both deal making and athletes
lives in the process.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.503
Hi.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.219
This is tough decision,

00:02:15.219 --> 00:02:16.470
but I know that I've made the right one,

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.923
and I really feel it in my
heart and I can't deny it.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.603
And so I retire from the
great game of football today.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.150
Lots of players have managed to move

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.680
from the locker room to the boardroom,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.100
taking largely titular roles
as advisors and rainmakers

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.110
to companies or investment firms.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.780
Few can say they've committed
as much as Steve Young,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.220
studying investment memos

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.400
the same way he studied
the Niners playbook.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.360
All right, dental?

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.680
Pat?

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.730
You're on your horse.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.080
Young's firm, HGGC sits

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.690
in one of the most fascinating
corners of Wall Street,

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:52.950
private equity.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.520
Once upon a time, that
industry was relatively hidden.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.740
Now it's one of the
most influential engines

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.210
of the global economy.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.940
It feels like anecdotally,
I'm hearing more like,

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.470
oh, private equity,
consolidated, this practice...

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.350
Talking about acquiring dental practices

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.790
may not be as captivating

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.770
as throwing game-winning touchdowns,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.310
but it's a fast growing
very lucrative business.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.780
Collectively, private equity firms

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.570
control more than $6 trillion in assets,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.160
according to McKenzie.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.600
The founders of the biggest firms,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.850
guys like Henry Kravis
and Steve Schwartzman,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:29.910
they are billionaires many times over.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.320
So let's talk for a minute

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.840
about the basics of private equity.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.970
Think of it like buying
and selling houses.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.390
Basically, PE firms use a
mix of cash from investors

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.740
and money borrowed from banks

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.350
to buy companies, make
them more profitable,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.510
and sell them down the line.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:46.650
And just like with houses,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.540
some people own them for a few years,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.860
take really good care of them,

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.930
and sell them for a nice profit.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.850
Others, they're more like flippers,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.580
who look to do the minimum
to make a quick buck.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.890
Buyout firms have been rightly
criticized over the years

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:00.780
for shuttering plants and cutting jobs

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.360
in pursuit of those massive profits.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:04.590
Especially in recent years,

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.120
when it comes to medical practices

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.100
owned by private equity firms.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.990
Some practitioners have complained

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.620
that it forces them to choose
between profits and patients.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.490
So as they've grown in stature,
so too has the scrutiny,

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.090
and the sense of responsibility
beyond the bottom line.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.790
Young and his partners,
they're more than cognizant

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.650
of that historical reputation.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.010
And they're aiming to be more
nurturing than swashbuckling.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.690
They're operating at a smaller scale

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.210
than the big firms in the
so-called middle market,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:37.320
buying companies worth $50
million to $500 million.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.180
That's still a pretty big business.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:44.180
HGGC, they've raised four funds,
totaling almost $7 billion.

00:04:44.339 --> 00:04:46.717
For Steve Young, it's unquestionably

00:04:46.717 --> 00:04:48.060
one of the most successful

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.250
second acts of any former athlete.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.210
And while Steve may live

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.740
in the same neighborhood in Silicon Valley

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.180
as Mark Zuckerberg,

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.980
you might not know it from his ride.

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.200
Vans are the best cars on the road.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.213
If you don't have a van and you have kids,

00:05:03.213 --> 00:05:04.860
and you don't have a van, you're an idiot.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.090
Yeah.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:10.560
I mean, how do you not go 14
cup holders and six outlets?

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.563
I can run everything right
here, it's like a rocket ship.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.150
87 was when I lived here.
Yeah.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.230
I moved here for the first time.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.680
Silicon Valley was kind of up
to full speed in ten years.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.344
So by 1997.
Yeah.

00:05:24.344 --> 00:05:27.300
So in the last 20 years
or so, 25 years even,

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.336
it's changed in that the
biggest companies in the world

00:05:31.336 --> 00:05:34.530
that weren't here 20
years ago are now here.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.036
Right.

00:05:36.036 --> 00:05:37.560
But what hasn't changed is
Stanford's down the street.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:38.880
Silicon Valley's always been

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.193
kind of a entrepreneurial place.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.980
Back when Steve was playing
in the early nineties,

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.641
the technology boom was
really just cranking up,

00:05:49.641 --> 00:05:51.900
just a few miles from where the 49ers

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.420
played at Candlestick Park.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:55.590
We're watching Silicon Valley

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.930
just explode around us in Santa Clara.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:01.470
ThreeCom, Cisco, name any
business, any big business.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.180
And I don't know who had the first idea.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.280
I can't give it to anybody,
'cause I don't know who said it.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.469
Was like, let's bring
'em in the locker room

00:06:07.469 --> 00:06:08.640
and we'll tell 'em look,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.350
we'll trade some access
to the locker room,

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:11.370
'cause that's what we have.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:12.797
Right.

00:06:12.797 --> 00:06:13.630
'Cause everyone wanted that, right?

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:14.730
The 49ers at that time?
Yeah.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.410
It'd be like right now who name it?

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.650
Like the Patriots or the
Warriors, the Rams or anyone else.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.871
And so we had ten, 15 different companies,

00:06:22.871 --> 00:06:25.890
$10,000, $15,000 each, we split it all up,

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.593
and that we just started
to come pretty regularly.

00:06:29.869 --> 00:06:31.410
Those investments and relationships

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.540
were critical for Steve's own transition

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.250
into the world of investing,

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.380
but access to money
and deals while playing

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.035
isn't a guarantee of financial success.

00:06:40.035 --> 00:06:41.370
Going into private
equity was a windy road.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.500
When you're playing, you're
one of the best in the world,

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:44.430
or best athletes in the world,

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.637
but you don't have financial literacy.

00:06:46.637 --> 00:06:48.451
But you have all this money.
Yeah.

00:06:48.451 --> 00:06:50.888
And so there's a big, huge chasm

00:06:50.888 --> 00:06:53.190
in between your financial literacy

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.480
and the money that's in the bank.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.360
We've watched those stories
and the tragic stories.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:58.387
Yeah.

00:06:58.387 --> 00:06:59.580
It was a painful moment

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.650
because when you have somebody coming

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.420
and trying to take your Rose Bowl rings,

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.850
your trophies, your cars, your house.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:07.980
Redskin's running back Clinton Portis

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.500
earning more than $40
million on the gridiron,

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.870
but life after football led to bankruptcy

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.643
and almost drove him to murder.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.203
Around 15% of NFL players
filed for bankruptcy

00:07:19.203 --> 00:07:22.320
within 25 years of retirement.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.345
And while there are extreme
examples of failure and success,

00:07:25.345 --> 00:07:28.413
every player has the same transition.

00:07:29.869 --> 00:07:33.000
For me, it started when
I first got here in '93,

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.200
'cause I wasn't drafted.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.810
So being, you know, kind of coming in,

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:38.960
it was like every day
could be my last day.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.510
While Junior Bryant played
for ten seasons in the NFL,

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.580
his transition was sudden.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.590
While making a tackle,
Junior went airborne,

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.723
landing with this full weight on his head.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.490
Most careers kind of end
like mine, unfortunately.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.840
It's just the nature of the game.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.390
And even though I was doing all that work

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.750
from the very first day,
I still wasn't ready.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:03.120
I kind of really wandered for a few years.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.038
I had ideas of things I wanted to do.

00:08:05.038 --> 00:08:06.720
But I just didn't take the step,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.340
just to kind of move forward.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:09.840
I had the same paralysis.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.180
Like what exactly am I gonna do?

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.460
What am I even qualified to do?

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.820
Junior would eventually end
up working at HGGC with Steve,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.260
as well as serve on the board

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.780
for his foundation Forever Young.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.100
Through his foundation Forever Young,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.600
Steve is looking to bridge that gap

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.100
between athletes having a lot of money,

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.980
and as he describes, financial literacy.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.600
He hopes to create a repository

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.872
of player transition stories.

00:08:32.872 --> 00:08:35.448
It's a resource for players by players

00:08:35.448 --> 00:08:39.720
to give unbiased advice on
what comes after sports.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.690
An effort to pay forward
his success off the field

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.100
and help others transition

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.290
from the field to executive suites,

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.810
like the one his firm
has at Levi's Stadium.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.593
Right, so this is the suite.

00:08:51.593 --> 00:08:53.370
This is the firm suite.
This is the suite.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.304
Playing the game, and
what we all been through,

00:08:55.304 --> 00:08:58.470
players don't realize
how useful that can be

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.610
if you paid attention,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.800
They don't realize the
resource that they can be.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.050
As Junior said, we're all kind of frozen.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:05.010
And I want 'em to know,

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:06.540
by definition of what you've been through,

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.783
you have something to
add to the conversation.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:15.630
I began preparing for my
future beyond football

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.910
probably earlier than I wanted to,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.880
because my dad kept pecking at
me about the rest of my life.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:21.713
What are you gonna do?

00:09:21.713 --> 00:09:23.910
What's your plan, what's
your plan, what's your plan?

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.320
And I was like, my plan
is to play football.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.240
And he is like, that's not a plan.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.370
That motivation pushed
Steve to go back to school

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.990
at the same time he was playing,

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:34.140
running from Super Bowls to
law school for six years.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.500
In 1994, the same year he was league MVP,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:41.130
he also graduated with a law
degree from Brigham Young.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.570
Without the law degree,

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.000
I wouldn't have been able
to get the opportunity

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.200
to get into direct investing.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.450
Now, did I have the MBA that
everyone had out of Harvard?

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:51.210
Did I do the four years
of consulting or banking?

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:54.906
No, but I think I was
given the opportunity

00:09:54.906 --> 00:09:57.450
because I had that in my pocket.

00:09:57.450 --> 00:09:58.440
I wouldn't have gone to law school

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.750
if my dad had not driven it in my head

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:02.880
that you better have a plan.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.980
As his professional career wound down,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:08.190
he would use those relationships
forged in the locker room

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:10.680
to find ways into the world of business.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:12.192
He sat on the board of Power Bar

00:10:12.192 --> 00:10:15.390
after becoming a huge fan
of the product as a player.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.910
He used to just down
them on the sidelines,

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.250
Like Niners teammates Ronnie
Lott and Harris Barton,

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.280
Young had become more
invested in investing.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.810
He initially formed Northgate Capital

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:25.980
with some ex teammates,

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.990
and then went on to Sorenson Capital,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:31.740
where he first worked with his
future partner, Rich Lawson.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:34.210
I actually started my
career at Morgan Stanley,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:35.730
prior to business school,

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.310
in the mergers and
acquisitions department,

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:40.320
and slept under a desk in New York

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.660
and in Tokyo, Japan for Morgan Stanley.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.190
We had started as direct investors

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:45.780
at a firm called Sorenson Capital,

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.810
and that was probably 20 years ago.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:50.910
The two eventually decided
to create their own firm

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:53.850
alongside two high caliber,
private equity mentors,

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.490
Bob Gay and John Huntsman Sr.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.810
What looked like a winning partnership

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:58.830
among the four of them,

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:02.430
initially called Huntsman Gay
Global Capital, didn't last.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.440
Gay opted to take a job
with the Mormon Church

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.270
and Huntsman, who planned to join

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.580
after his company was
bought, never joined,

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.130
when that deal to sell
his company fell through.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:14.160
So no Huntsman, no Gay,
but Young and Lawson

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.580
opted to press ahead as HGGC.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:20.681
So this is the inner sanctum.

00:11:20.681 --> 00:11:22.260
I like it.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.216
We did not clean it up for you.

00:11:24.216 --> 00:11:26.703
I apologize, this is a working day.

00:11:28.227 --> 00:11:30.323
That's great, and I do, I love the helmet.

00:11:30.323 --> 00:11:32.010
The firm's offices are filled
with sports memorabilia,

00:11:32.010 --> 00:11:34.110
much of which is used for
prizes and charity work

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.930
that Steve and the firm
participate in year round.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.160
But Steve is quick to point out

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.900
that it took a while for him to embrace

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.030
being surrounded by his past.

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:46.200
When I retired from football,
Roger Staubach was my hero,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.781
and we developed a relationship,
and I asked him, Roger,

00:11:48.781 --> 00:11:51.810
you are one of the
greatest transition guys

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.570
in the history of the NFL,
to go do something else.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.130
Give me some tips.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:58.710
And his number one tip was run.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.360
And I go, run, what do you mean run?

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.220
He goes, just get it, run, away.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.053
Yeah.

00:12:03.053 --> 00:12:06.150
And but he said, because
it'll never leave you.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.300
You'll always have football
and the history in the NFL

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.540
and all the things that
happened, but you need to run.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.603
You also, and I don't
think this is a stretch,

00:12:15.603 --> 00:12:19.144
had some hesitation about

00:12:19.144 --> 00:12:23.190
being ex-football player investor.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:24.720
You wanted to be an investor.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:25.553
Is that fair?

00:12:25.553 --> 00:12:26.998
That's very fair.

00:12:26.998 --> 00:12:30.060
And I think the more
famous you are as a player,

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.590
the more accomplished you are,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:35.128
the more I thought the difficulty would be

00:12:35.128 --> 00:12:36.870
to actually have people
take you seriously.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:38.970
And I wanted my fame to be a tool.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:40.710
I didn't want it to be a weapon,

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.210
and it's so easy to weaponize, right?

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:43.800
You can bring in and just slay the room,

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.510
and that's not the way I wanted to do it.

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:47.043
But certainly when I started,

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.760
I held myself back in some ways.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:55.470
So integral to that transition
as it were, that evolution,

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.120
is Rich Lawson, your partner.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.700
And we actually, I talked to him last week

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:00.600
about this very topic.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.681
So I wanna show you something,
All right.

00:13:02.681 --> 00:13:04.500
His take on it.

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.510
Bring some drama.

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.629
We have a very long hallway
leading to our shared office,

00:13:09.629 --> 00:13:12.270
and I can't seem to get my own wall.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:15.150
And every week I would put up his jerseys

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:17.280
from his various different accolades,

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.280
the USFL or the 49ers or BYU.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:22.650
And it would be strange because
one day they'd be there,

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:24.387
and then I would come back from a trip

00:13:24.387 --> 00:13:26.040
and they'd all be gone.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.080
So I think for years he wanted to minimize

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.260
what his past was, as
opposed to embrace it.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.970
It's been really critical to our business.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:35.430
It's given us a bit of a
defining characteristic

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.110
in addition to being good investors,

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:40.230
because no matter how successful
you are in other venues,

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:42.810
if you're not delivering great
returns for your investors,

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:43.983
it doesn't matter.

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:47.940
All true, every bit of it.

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:51.690
I'll call it immaturity on my part,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:56.160
but it was born out of this
desire to try to make sure

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.290
that what I was doing,

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:01.110
I wanted people to know
I was serious about.

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:03.570
And that meant grinding it out on deals,

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:06.450
doing the work to find,
buy, and fix companies.

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:10.740
HGGC has completed more than
500 portfolio investments,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.193
transactions with an enterprise
value of nearly $50 billion.

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.590
Young and Lawson's playbook?

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.500
It's one of cultivating
relationships over long periods

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.270
to consummate deals.

00:14:21.270 --> 00:14:23.250
As time went on, the sports memorabilia

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:25.230
and Wall Street deal toys,

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.030
those lucite mementos of companies

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.690
bought and sold and taken
public, began to share space.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:32.940
You can see the 49er room
is the main conference room.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:34.685
Okay, we get it.

00:14:34.685 --> 00:14:36.120
Someone joked, I said, we
promise to put you over Joe

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.920
is the only place I ever get.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:38.753
It's funny,

00:14:38.753 --> 00:14:40.830
where I was sneaking
around your office earlier,

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:43.950
I saw that picture of you and Montana.

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:46.080
Very intimate portrait.

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.060
We all agree that it was awkward.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.230
It never wasn't awkward.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:51.780
In fact, AT&amp;T did a
commercial on it recently.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:52.613
Exactly.

00:14:52.613 --> 00:14:54.030
But it was always profitable.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:56.045
And I think that was that creative tension

00:14:56.045 --> 00:14:57.660
that had in a part of it.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:14:59.970
And I'll always honor my time with Joe.

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:03.513
I hated watching, but
I loved watching Joe.

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.030
Do you think about that in the context

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:10.080
of like business and CEOs
and succession, like.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.060
Oh, hundred percent.
You've got to, right?

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:15.453
So much of this business
is classic relationship.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:22.680
A few days later, I joined Steve

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.845
at his charity golf
tournament at Troon North,

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:27.990
just outside of Scottsdale, Arizona.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.370
The proceeds of that day

00:15:29.370 --> 00:15:32.760
go to his philanthropic
organization Forever Young.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.340
For nearly three decades,
that's served children

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:36.780
facing significant physical,

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:38.850
emotional, and financial challenges.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.950
I love golf, I love thinking about it.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:42.870
I just don't play it very often,

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.850
but I believe that if you love golf

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:45.960
and you don't play very often,

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:49.380
you can get better kind
of in your backyard.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.173
That's my theory, and
it has proven untrue.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:00.000
Steve's ability on the course

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.130
may not be etched into any record books,

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.630
but following him around all day,

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:06.750
it's clear that it's
not for lack of trying.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:07.583
Oh well.

00:16:09.319 --> 00:16:13.260
All right, off we go.

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.030
We were constantly hustling behind him

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:18.030
as he caught up with executives
in the portfolio companies

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.670
and reminisced with former
and current players.

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:21.510
He's a day trader.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.360
He taught himself, self-made day trader.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:28.080
And so he's on the bus
after a game in New England,

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.700
and he's day trading on the bus,

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:33.630
and Bill Belichick sees him
and cuts him on the spot.

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.672
The season that they
went to the Super Bowl,

00:16:35.672 --> 00:16:39.693
day trading got him cut.

00:16:40.868 --> 00:16:42.803
You know, in many ways
it's a convention, right,

00:16:42.803 --> 00:16:44.280
Of the people that I don't
see, maybe once a year,

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.140
we're half the field or more

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:48.690
from our own portfolio in our own firm.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:50.400
And so it's kind of all come together.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:53.880
My firm, our charity,
our fundraising efforts,

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.820
and then the friends that are
around, my dad and my brother,

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:58.260
it's all happens right there

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:00.835
as a confluence of all
these parts of my life

00:17:00.835 --> 00:17:03.630
that have found its common center.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:04.470
You should meet Andy.

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:05.760
This is Andy in the black hat here.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:07.830
He's a CEO.
It may sound trite,

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:10.020
but these personal
relationships and conversations

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:12.540
that Steve has with
folks on the golf course,

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.610
it's actually similar
to his and the firm's

00:17:14.610 --> 00:17:15.990
approach to private equity.

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:18.210
For them, the art is in the relationships

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:20.220
built across the negotiating table.

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.860
And even in the days, months, decades

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:25.050
leading up to being at that table,

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:26.850
rather than Excel based financial models

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:30.000
ground out by young analysts
in Manhattan skyscrapers

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.730
more typically associated
with buyout firms.

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:35.555
If you could leave the hubris behind,

00:17:35.555 --> 00:17:38.340
that says, I love that business,

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.460
but it would be so much
better if I ran it, right.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.760
To me, the more profitable way to do it,

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:48.434
not just in returns,
but in kind of legacy,

00:17:48.434 --> 00:17:50.820
in abundance,

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.550
is to approach it kind of looking at

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:56.790
the person across the table
as your strategic partner.

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:58.920
Help me understand how
you fit into the family?

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:02.400
One of HGGC's best partnerships
was with Brian Adams,

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.950
the founder and CEO of an
insurance technology company

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:06.690
called Integrity.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:08.670
Well, so we had an advisor

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:10.920
that linked us up in the beginning,

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:12.435
and you know, the first thing,

00:18:12.435 --> 00:18:15.210
everybody thinks of Steve
as a football player,

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.610
and you're intimidated by this idea

00:18:17.610 --> 00:18:19.590
of this Hall of Fame quarterback.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:21.720
But what you really see quickly

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:25.376
is how humble Steve is, how normal he is.

00:18:25.376 --> 00:18:28.380
Integrity is part of an auction in 2016,

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:31.050
one where 19 others were bidding.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:34.784
And while HGGC didn't put up
the highest monetary offer,

00:18:34.784 --> 00:18:38.370
it was the relationship that
Steve had struck up with Brian

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.410
that helped them win the deal.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.150
Steve and I got to know each other

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:43.908
and got to become friends.

00:18:43.908 --> 00:18:47.100
And we started really kind
of aligning our values.

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:50.227
For us, it was a lot of this
kind of value alignment.

00:18:50.227 --> 00:18:54.180
It was like, we've got to figure
out how to become partners.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:56.733
And it's been an incredible rise for us.

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:00.930
After HGGC took a minority
stake in Integrity,

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:03.030
Steve was appointed chairman of the board.

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:05.970
In six years, the company grew
from a few hundred employees

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:08.070
to more than 5,000.

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:11.640
That's in large part due
to acquiring 125 companies

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.200
through M and A.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:15.937
Most recently Integrity
got a new round of funding,

00:19:15.937 --> 00:19:18.660
$1.2 billion from Silver Lake,

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:20.640
one of the biggest firms out there.

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.890
They returned the fund and more,

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:24.390
with one, just one investment

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:27.360
off of a faith and a relationship,

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:28.560
really more than anything.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.240
And a belief in each other.

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.070
Integrity is also an interesting example

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:35.370
of something notable, and
again, somewhat radical

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:38.730
that you do around
compensation and ownership,

00:19:38.730 --> 00:19:41.280
and something that you've
extended that idea,

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.280
even into your own firm
in a different way.

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:45.600
That's right.
So walk me through that.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:47.910
Brian had the idea that,

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:50.264
he had 3000 employees at the time,

00:19:50.264 --> 00:19:52.080
and you know what?

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:54.330
The equity owners of the business,

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:56.510
the people that have the stock,

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:59.313
are making incredible amounts of money,

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.661
but the employees are
getting paid their salaries.

00:20:02.661 --> 00:20:05.943
What if we gave them stock?

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.360
In 2019, Integrity would do something

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:13.290
almost unheard of for a company
of their size and potential.

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.990
They distributed partial
ownership in the company

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:17.223
to their employee base.

00:20:18.480 --> 00:20:20.790
He did that as a private business,

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:22.147
and I thought that's a way to solve

00:20:22.147 --> 00:20:26.010
for a lot of the ills of corporate world

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:27.990
and how people see the world,

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.990
as building equity
inside the employee base.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:31.980
And I loved that,

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:33.600
and I think we've done
it in our firm as well.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:35.670
And kind of more distributed partnership

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:38.340
and trying to give equity out.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:41.430
Ownership, not just held by two people,

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:42.263
because what that does

00:20:42.263 --> 00:20:46.563
is it allows for generational mindset.

00:20:48.150 --> 00:20:50.610
Steve's work with growing
players into business people

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.130
mirrors the work he's
doing at his own firm.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:55.630
There, he and Lawson are nurturing

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:57.960
the next generation of investors at HGGC.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:00.240
And they're thinking of
how his legacy will live on

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:01.770
in the world of private equity,

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:04.140
through those younger executives.

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:04.973
This is a place

00:21:04.973 --> 00:21:08.220
where there are 25ish
investment professionals,

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:09.480
and every single one of us

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:12.420
is welcome to bring
our ideas to the table.

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:13.710
And those ideas are heard.

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:16.830
So we meet every Friday
for investment committee,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.140
and it's not just the
investment committee.

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:21.480
It's not the five or
ten partners that meet,

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.160
it's the entire firm.

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:25.740
And a lot of places, that's not the case.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:29.520
Good friend ran a famous
private equity firm nearby.

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:31.410
And he said, your culture

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.360
is what I want our firm to be like?

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:34.470
Well, what do you do?

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:36.180
I said, well, the first
thing you gotta do,

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:38.760
is your founders need to put

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:40.410
all of their relationships in the middle

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:42.180
and no one owns anything.

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:43.740
And you don't fight over
those relationships.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:44.913
They're the firm's.

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:50.160
15 seconds, and he's like,
yeah, that's not gonna work.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:51.720
He's like, uh, I'm out.

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:54.953
So it's like, the culture has
to be kind of in the roots,

00:21:54.953 --> 00:21:57.153
and that resonates with people.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:02.730
Passing down this spirit of abundance

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:03.810
approach to private equity,

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:05.700
all came from Steve's playing days,

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.043
and his Hall of Fame coach, Bill Walsh.

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:11.700
I watched him do it in the late eighties.

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:13.380
There's a guy with a
camera, the old school,

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.120
you know, big thing of on his back

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:16.560
and he'd follow Bill around everywhere.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.330
Filming Bill as he spoke to the team,

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:19.560
went out on the practice field,

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:22.290
installed the offense, in the lunchroom.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.330
And I kept asking, what is this,

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:25.380
like a museum they're doing?

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.060
Or what are they doing,
a special documentary?

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:28.620
Like, no one knew.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:29.790
Well, what it was,

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.940
was that he was creating
a physical toolkit

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:36.480
of video based, audio
based, of all his speeches,

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.000
and all of his playbooks and
all of his installations,

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.770
everything paper based that he had,

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:43.050
and essentially wrapping
it all up as a toolkit

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:46.110
to hand it to his assistant coaches,

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:48.900
that he wanted to go have
success in the league.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.910
Who takes all their secrets,

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.320
all the things that makes them great

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.150
at the height of their greatness,

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.530
three or four generations ahead

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:57.180
of everybody else in the league,

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:58.437
and wraps it up and hands it

00:22:58.437 --> 00:23:02.730
to the people who are leaving
to go coach other places

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:04.050
and then says to them,

00:23:04.050 --> 00:23:06.180
you know what, I'll see you
in the championship game.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.290
And it happened.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.300
There's a spirit to legacy.

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:10.710
There's a spirit to...

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.930
And so I watched it and I said to him,

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:14.700
Bill, why would you do that?

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:18.030
And he said, Steve, we've
got to give opportunity.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:20.760
I want these men to succeed.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.470
And I thought, that's
a heck of a statement,

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:26.370
'cause of course you do,
but who does the actual work

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:28.470
to give them the opportunity to go succeed

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:31.590
and giving away what you
own that is most valuable?

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:33.150
Is that great business?

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:35.100
You can make an argument

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:38.523
that maybe that is the great
business that we need to be in.

